# Poverty, Inequality, and Unemployment

> Poverty is lack of the minimum needed to live; inequality is how
> unevenly what exists is spread; unemployment is labour left idle —
> three failures a growing GDP alone cannot fix.

[definition]
**Absolute poverty** means income too low to meet a defined minimum
of basic needs (food, shelter, clothing). **Relative poverty**
means income far below the society's average — the poor of rich
countries are relatively, not absolutely, poor.
[/definition]

## 1. Measuring Poverty in India

Poverty lines were set by expert committees, each revising the
methodology:

| Committee | Year | Approach |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Alagh | 1979 | Calorie norm — 2,400 rural / 2,100 urban per capita/day |
| Lakdawala | 1993 | Retained calories; expenditure-based poverty line |
| Tendulkar | 2009 | Mixed reference — private consumption of goods with implicit norms |
| Rangarajan | 2014 | Higher spending norm: about ₹972 rural / ₹1,407 urban per month |

Based on the Tendulkar line, India's poverty ratio fell from about
45% in 1993–94 to about 22% in 2011-12 — roughly 27 crore people
exiting poverty in two decades of fast growth.

## 2. Multidimensional Poverty (MPI)

The **Multidimensional Poverty Index**, developed by UNDP with the
**Oxford Poverty and Human Development Initiative (OPHI)**, measures
poverty in **three dimensions** with ten indicators:

- **Health** — nutrition, child mortality
- **Education** — years of schooling, school attendance
- **Living standards** — cooking fuel, sanitation, drinking water,
  electricity, housing, household assets

A household is poor if the weighted sum of deprivations crosses a
threshold — income never appears; deprivations do.

## 3. Causes and Remedies

Causes: low income growth for the bottom, unemployment, poor
education and health, assetlessness, and social exclusion.

Flagship anti-poverty programmes:

| Programme | Year | Core idea |
| --- | --- | --- |
| MGNREGA | 2005 | Legal guarantee of 100 days of wage employment per rural household |
| NRLM (Ajeevika) | 2011 | Self-help groups, credit, livelihoods |
| NFSA | 2013 | Right to subsidised food grains — two-thirds of population |
| PM Awas / Jan Dahan | 2015+ | Housing for the poor; universal bank accounts |

## 4. Inequality: Lorenz Curve and Gini

The **Lorenz curve** plots cumulative income share against cumulative
population share (from poorest up); the farther it sags below the
45° line of equality, the more unequal the distribution. The **Gini
coefficient** summarises it:

@math
Gini = \frac{Area\ between\ diagonal\ and\ Lorenz\ curve}{Total\ area\ under\ diagonal}
@/math

A Gini of **0** is perfect equality; **1** is one person owning
everything. India's consumption Gini sits around the mid-0.3s —
moderate by cross-country standards but rising with growth.

## 5. Types of Unemployment

| Type | Meaning | Typical example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Structural | Skills mismatch — jobs exist, skills don't | Old weaver when mills automate |
| Frictional | Between jobs while searching | Engineer switching cities |
| Cyclical | Demand-deficient, follows downturns | Construction layoffs in recession |
| Seasonal | Work only part of the year | Farm labour, tourism workers |
| Disguised | More workers than needed — MP near zero | Family members on a small farm |
| Open | Willing, seeking, getting nothing | Educated job-seeker unemployed |

## 6. Measuring Unemployment in India

The Periodic Labour Force Survey uses reference periods:

- **Usual Status (US)** — activity over the past year; captures the
  long-term picture (lowest rate, includes marginal work).
- **Current Weekly Status (CWS)** — activity over the past week.
- **Current Daily Status** — each day of the week counted.

[important title="Disguised unemployment hides in plain sight"]
Five family members working a plot that two could farm are not
recorded as unemployed — they *appear* employed, yet their marginal
product is near zero. Shifting one out loses nothing, which is why
surplus farm labour can migrate to industry without hurting output.
[/important]

## 7. Quick Revision Table

| Question | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| First poverty line committee | Alagh, 1979 — calorie norms |
| Latest spending-norm committee | Rangarajan, 2014 |
| Tendulkar trend | ~45% (1993-94) → ~22% (2011-12) poverty ratio |
| MPI dimensions | Health, education, living standards |
| MGNREGA | 2005 — 100 days guaranteed rural wage work |
| Gini range | 0 (equal) to 1 (max inequality) |
| Disguised unemployment | Marginal product ≈ zero |
| Usual Status | One-year reference period |

[summary]
India's poverty line evolved from calories (Alagh, Lakdawala) to
consumption norms (Tendulkar, Rangarajan), while the MPI counts
deprivations directly. Inequality is read off the Lorenz curve as a
Gini between 0 and 1, and unemployment comes in six types — with
disguised unemployment the classic Indian farm puzzle that Usual
Status and weekly reference periods help uncover.
[/summary]
